First, we regress annual mean temperature $\bar y_s$ on calendar year $s$ by OLS,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:trend-annual}
  \bar y_s = \alpha + \beta\,(s - s_0) + u_s ,
\end{equation}
with Newey-West (HAC) standard errors based on a Bartlett kernel and bandwidth
\begin{equation}\label{eq:hac-lag}
  L = \bigl\lfloor 4\,(T/100)^{2/9} \bigr\rfloor ,
\end{equation}
where $T$ is the number of observations in the regression. The slope is reported as $10\hat\beta$ in degrees Celsius per decade. Second, we repeat this on the deseasonalised monthly series $\tilde y_t = y_t - \hat\mu_{m(t)}$, where $\hat\mu_m$ is the mean of calendar month $m$ in the sample used, regressing $\tilde y_t$ on elapsed time in years.

We also test whether the amplitude and phase of the annual harmonic drift linearly over time. With elapsed time $\tau_t$ in years, we estimate
\begin{equation}\label{eq:drift}
  y_t = a + b\,\tau_t + (c_1 + d_1\tau_t)\cos(2\pi\tau_t) + (c_2 + d_2\tau_t)\sin(2\pi\tau_t) + u_t ,
\end{equation}
so that the amplitude at time $\tau$ is $A(\tau)=\sqrt{(c_1+d_1\tau)^2+(c_2+d_2\tau)^2}$ and the phase is $\phi(\tau)=\operatorname{atan2}(c_2+d_2\tau,\,c_1+d_1\tau)$. The joint Wald test of $H_0\colon d_1=d_2=0$ uses HAC standard errors with the lag from Equation~\eqref{eq:hac-lag}.

